WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.475
This is the Daily Tech Brief for Wednesday, 21 October.

00:00:04.175 --> 00:00:16.250
Top headlines: SA-Solver unlocked in ComfyUI beats Euler at 10 steps. MOSS-Image vision text encode allows reference photos in, from-scratch generation out. MOSS-Image climbs the Hugging Face trending page again.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:26.250
Later, a closer look at SA-Solver unlocked in ComfyUI beats Euler at 10 steps, and MOSS-Image vision text encode: reference photos in, from-scratch generation out.

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:50.425
SA-Solver unlocked in ComfyUI beats Euler at 10 steps. A write-up from the low-step camp, covering 8–14 steps on Flux, SD3, and Anima, shows stochastic strength eta=1.7 beating plain Euler at 10 steps. The post documents the settings that did not work alongside the successful configuration. According to the thread, this offers a direct quality lever for batch image generation that is currently budget-pinned to short sampler chains.

00:00:51.125 --> 00:01:13.700
MOSS-Image vision text encode: reference photos in, from-scratch generation out. A ComfyUI node plus workflow runs a reference photo through the text encoder's vision side. Per the author, the embeddings are extracted upfront, so the KSampler pays no extra compute. The reference steers pose, lighting, and scene of a freshly generated image rather than editing an existing one. In the thread, users request an identity LoRA like Krea 2's.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:22.475
The author is direct that it is not an editor; the photo is a visual reference for new generation. Some users are already running it as a two-step detailer pass.

00:01:23.175 --> 00:01:46.250
MOSS-Image climbs the Hugging Face trending page again. The Apache-2.0 open-weights family is still climbing. It is up from 300 stars on 19 Oct, first featured 19 Oct, to 450 stars for the base model. The SFT checkpoint and Comfy-Org's single-file ComfyUI build are trending right beside it. Since the 19 Oct count grew, there are 7 new adapters, 5 fine-tunes, and 3 quants. There is a fresh wish list discussion on the model page.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:56.700
Today's trending mentions cover the SFT checkpoint, the Comfy-Org build, and lodestones/Kromoss. This is the same ecosystem the ComfyUI node and box workflows already circulate.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:16.725
WebGPU tool turns orbit videos into Gaussian splats entirely in the browser. gausify-orbit converts orbit videos, including AI-generated ones, into Gaussian splats 100% locally in the browser with WebGPU. According to the project, this makes it the shortest path from a turntable clip to a splat asset before touching the training and runtime sides.

00:02:17.425 --> 00:02:39.250
First complete engine fuses Gaussian splatting and neural inference in Vulkan. Started a year ago as a Vulkan learning project, it runs splat rasterization and neural inference in the same engine. It keeps its command buffers alive instead of rebuilding them. This is the runtime-side companion to the training-side densification fix. In the thread, commenters ask how changing work sizes as the camera moves are handled.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:48.000
The author says not recreating the buffers was a design goal from the start, to minimize CPU-GPU interaction and possibly ease distributed computing later.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:03:08.525
Gaussian head model builds photoreal generalizable heads from a single image. EGGHead goes straight to a Gaussian representation from one input image. This avoids mesh pipelines that trade photorealism against generalizability and efficiency. According to the paper posted to arXiv, this is the most direct 2D→3D shortcut when you need a character head without a scan.

00:03:09.225 --> 00:03:24.375
Fumaris: GPU smoke and fire simulation inside Blender. Venous FX's Fumaris puts the sim on the GPU. More usefully, it lets you nudge parameters while the preview is still running. This skips the stop, tune, restart loop that normally makes smoke and fire tuning slow.

00:03:25.075 --> 00:03:36.600
Unreal Engine 5.8.4 hotfix released. The patch lands on the same 5.8 line the Fallout port is playing on an early build of. It is the maintenance step for anyone moving that engine's tooling forward.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:04:01.800
In brief. BRIA.ai releases Fibo 2.0, an image edit model. The subreddit was onto it within a day, with a custom LoRA trained a day after release, old-photo restoration and colorization tests, and an edit turbo variant. That fast fine-tune wave mirrors the MOSS-Image derivative surge, though the early Fibo LoRAs keep subjects photorealistic and miss the target artwork's style. HEISS UI: free, open-source ComfyUI frontend with no workflows for MOSS and Flux3.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:25.150
The frontend serves MOSS and Flux3 with no workflows to load at all. It is the no-setup front door onto the same image models the trending checkpoint family covers, useful when you want to evaluate them without first building a ComfyUI graph. Occlusion-aware densification for 3DGS scores Gaussians by contribution, not by views. It targets adaptive density control by counting how much each splat actually contributes rather than how many views it appears in.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:49.000
It is a drop-in candidate for anyone still growing scenes the vanilla way. YMA Painterly: 12 shaders for a painterly look in C4D and Houdini. Theo Daley's 12-shader pack drops straight into C4D or Houdini scenes. The write-up walks through how he wires the shaders rather than just linking a download. Fallout 3 ported to UE 5.8 by streaming its original assets live, with no re-export. A decade-long port playtest shows a working pipeline for legacy games.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:57.225
Instead of dumping a converted asset set, the engine reads straight from a local Fallout 3 install, so anything not remade still plays from the source files.

00:04:57.925 --> 00:05:00.375
Now, a closer look at today's top stories.

00:05:01.075 --> 00:05:20.975
A new post on Reddit details how the SA-Solver in ComfyUI can outperform standard Euler sampling at 10 steps. The discussion focuses on users generating images with models like Flux, SD3, and Anima at low step counts between 8 and 14. According to the post, standard Euler samplers produce waxy surfaces, while ancestral samplers cause anatomical drift.

00:05:20.975 --> 00:05:42.675
The SA-Solver, originally from a paper by Xue et al. at NeurIPS 2023, uses an Adams predictor-corrector on drift while keeping diffusion separate. It costs exactly 1 NFE per step. The key improvement involves adjusting the stochastic strength, or eta, to 1.7. Previously, ComfyUI hardcoded this value to 1.0 within a fixed 20% to 80% window, offering no user controls.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:06:05.525
By unlocking this setting, the solver provides better texture and anatomy without increasing the step count. This is particularly useful for batch image generation that is currently limited by budget constraints requiring short sampler chains. The post also documents settings that did not work, providing a clear path for optimization. Users in the comments expressed interest, with one noting they use Anima and plan to test the configuration immediately.

00:06:06.225 --> 00:06:22.475
A new ComfyUI node for MOSS-Image allows users to feed reference photos directly through the Qwen3.5-9B vision tower. According to the project page, this leaves the embeddings in the text conditioning so the image conditions generation as part of the prompt rather than as a VAE latent.

00:06:22.475 --> 00:06:33.725
The author explains that because these vision embeddings are extracted upfront by the text encoder before generation starts, the process adds zero compute overhead to the KSampler and runs at native speed.

00:06:34.425 --> 00:06:55.475
The tool is designed to generate a new image from scratch using a photo as a visual reference, rather than editing an existing one. In examples shared on Reddit, the model picked up pose, lighting, and scene details directly from the input photo, even when the text prompt was completely empty. Users can still use the prompt to fine-tune specific character features, but the reference photo remains the dominant guide.

00:06:55.475 --> 00:07:03.800
Some users report using it as a two-step detailer or upscaler, while others note that the workflow is very similar to ComfyUI-Krea2TextEncoder.

